	<title>Math211Fa24-FinalExam-PracticeB</title>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Clearly mark the correct answer(s) for each of the following by completely filling in the appropriate bubble. <term>No justification is needed.</term>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>(Multiple Choice-Choose one)</term> The reproduction function for salmon in a fishery is
				</p>
				<p>
					<m>f(p)=-\frac{1}{10}p^2+13p</m>, where <m>p</m> and <m>f(p)</m> are in the thousands. The maximum sustainable yield is which of the following?
				</p>
			</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>60</m> thousand
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>360</m> thousand
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>420</m> thousand
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								None of the above.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>(Multiple Choice-Choose one)</term> If <m>f(x,y)</m> has a critical point where <m>f_{xx}</m> and <m>f_{yy}</m> are both positive, then...
				</p>
			</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>f</m> must have a relative minimum at that point.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>f</m> must have a relative maximum at that point.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<m>f</m> must have saddle point at that point.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								There is not enough information to classify the critical point.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>(True/False)</term> If <m>C(x,y)</m> is the cost function for a soda factory for <m>x</m> units of Coca-Cola and <m>y</m> units of Sprite, <m>C_x(x,y)</m> is the marginal cost function for Sprite when the production of Coca-Cola is held constant.
				</p>
			</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								True
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								False
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>(True/False)</term> <m>\displaystyle\int_0^\infty \frac{1}{10000}dx</m> converges.
				</p>
			</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="no">
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								True
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								False
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<term>(Multiple Choice-Choose one)</term> Suppose a study indicates that the distribution of income for lawyers is given by the Lorenz curve <m>L(x)=x^2</m>. Suppose the Gini index for doctors is <m>0.5</m>. Which profession has the more equal distribution of income?
				</p>
			</statement>
				<choices multiple-correct="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p>
								Lawyers
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								Doctors
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								They are the same.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				The demand function <m>d(x)</m> and supply function <m>s(x)</m> for a particular commodity are given as: <me>d(x)=131-\frac{1}{3}x^2, \; \; s(x)=50+\frac{2}{3}x^2</me>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the consumers’ surplus at demand level <m>A=3</m>. You do not need to simplify your final numerical answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\amp d(3)=131-\frac{1}{3} \cdot 3^2=131-3=128 \\
						\amp \int_0^3\left(131-\frac{1}{3} x^2-128\right) d x=\int_0^3\left(3-1 / 3 x^2\right) d x \\
						\amp =3 x-\left.\frac{1}{9} x^3\right|_0 ^3 \\
						\amp =3 \cdot 3-\frac{1}{9} \cdot 3^3=9-3=6
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the market demand (the positive value of <m>x</m> at which the demand function intersects the supply function).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						300-\frac{1}{3} x^2 \amp =50+\frac{2}{3} x^2 \\
						81 \amp =x^2 \Rightarrow x=9
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the producers’ surplus at market demand. You do not need to simplify your final numerical answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						B \amp =s(9)=50+\frac{2}{3} \cdot 9^2=50+54=104 \\
						\text { Surp. } \amp =\int_0^9\left(104-\left(50+\frac{2}{3} x^2\right)\right) d x \\
						\amp \left.=\int_0^9 54-\frac{2}{3} x^2\right) d x=54 x-\left.\frac{2}{9} x^3\right|_0 ^9 \\
						\amp =54 \cdot 9-\frac{2}{9} \cdot 9^3=324
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Evaluate the following improper integral or show that it is divergent.
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>\displaystyle\int_0^\infty \dfrac{e^{2x}-e^{4x}}{e^{6x}}dx</m>
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\int_0^{\infty} \frac{e^{2 x}-e^{4 x}}{e^{6 x}} d x \amp =\int_0^{\infty}\left(\frac{e^{2 x}}{e^{6 x}}-\frac{e^{4 x}}{e^{6 x}}\right) d x \\
					\amp =\int_0^{\infty}\left(e^{-4 x}-e^{-2 x}\right) d x \\
					\amp =\lim _{b \rightarrow \infty} \int_0^b e^{-4 x}-e^{-2 x} d x \\
					\amp =\lim _{b \rightarrow \infty} \frac{e^{-4 x}}{-4}-\left.\frac{e^{-2 x}}{-2}\right|_0 ^b \\
					\amp =\lim _{b \rightarrow \infty} \frac{e^{-4 b}}{-4}+\frac{e^{-2 b}}{2}-\left(\frac{e^0}{-4}+\frac{e^0}{2}\right) \\
					\amp =0+0-\left(-\frac{1}{4}+\frac{1}{2}\right)=-\frac{1}{4}
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Calculate the derivative of each function. You do not need to simplify your answer.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x}+4\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{5}</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						f'(x)=\frac{-1}{x^2}+4 \cdot \frac{1}{2} x^{-1 / 2}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle g(x)=\ln(3x-x^2)</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						g'(x)=\frac{1}{3 x-x^2}(3-2 x)
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>F(x)=e^x(x^3+3x)</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						F'(x)=e^x\left(x^3+3 x\right)+e^x\left(3 x^2+3\right)
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>r(x)=\sqrt[3]{x^3+1}</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						r'(x)=\frac{1}{3}\left(x^3+1\right)^{-2 / 3}\left(3 x^2\right)
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Calculate each integral. You do not need to simplify your answer. Show all work.
			</p>
			<p>
				
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task workspace="1.75in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int x^2(1-4x^3)dx</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int x^2\left(1-4 x^3\right) d x \amp =\int\left(x^2-4 x^5\right) d x \\
						\amp =\frac{x^3}{3}-\frac{4 x^6}{6}+C
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int \frac{1-\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x}} dx</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\int \frac{1-\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt{x}} d x \amp =\int\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}-1\right) d x \\
						\amp =2x^{1 / 2}-x+C
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle\int \left(\dfrac{3}{2x}+e^{-x}\right)dx</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\int\left(\frac{3}{2 x}+e^{-x}\right) d x=\frac{3}{2} \ln |x|+\frac{e^{-x}}{-1}+C
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				The promoters of a state fair estimate that <m>t</m> hours after the gates open at 9:00am, visitors will be entering the fair at the rate of <m>N'(t)=16-t^2</m> hundred people per hour. Find the total number of people who will enter the fair between 11:00am and 2:00pm. You do not need to simplify the final numerical answer, but provide units. 
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\text { total } \amp =\int_2^5\left(16-t^2\right) d t=16 t-\left.\frac{t^3}{3}\right|_2^5 \\
					\amp =\left(16(5)-\frac{5^3}{3}\right)-\left(16(2)-\frac{2^3}{3}\right) \text { hundred people. }
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Set up and evaluate the integral to compute the shaded area (see below) bounded by the graphs <m>y=3-2x-x^2</m> and <m>y=x-1</m>. You do not need to simplify your final numerical answer.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Intersection:
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					3-2 x-x^2 \amp =x-1 \\
					0 \amp =x^2+3 x-4 \\
					\amp = (x-1)(x+4) \\
					x \amp =1,-4
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\text { Area } \amp =\int_{-4}^1\left[3-2 x-x^2-(x-1)\right] d x \\
					\amp =\int_{-4}^1\left[4-3 x-x^2\right] d x=\left.\left(4 x-\frac{3 x^2}{2}-\frac{x^3}{3}\right)\right|_{-4} ^1 \\
					\amp =4(1)-\frac{3(1)^2}{2}-\frac{1^3}{3}-\left(4(-4)-\frac{3(-4)^2}{2}-\frac{(-4)^3}{3}\right)
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				For the function <m>f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2-x^2y</m>:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task workspace="3in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute <m>f_{xx}, f_{yy},</m> and <m>f_{xy}</m>. Show all work.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{array}{ll}
						f_x=2 x-2 x y \amp f_{x x}=2-2 y \\
						f_y=4 y-x^2 \amp f_{y y}=4 \\
						\amp f_{x y}=-2 x
						\end{array}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The critical points of <m>f(x,y)</m> are <m>(0,0), (2,1)</m>, and <m>(-2,1)</m>. Determine, using the <m>D</m>-test, if each point corresponds to a relative maximum, relative minimum, or saddle point. Show all work.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						D=f_{x x} f_{y y}-\left(f_{x y}\right)^2 \amp =(2-2 y)(4)-(-2 x)^2 \\
						\amp =8-8 y-4 x^2
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					<me>
						D(0,0)=8 \gt 0 \rightarrow f_{x x}(0,0)=2 \gt 0 \rightarrow(0,0) \text{ is a rel. min.}
					</me>
					<me>
						D(2,1)=8-8 \cdot 1-4(2)^2=-16 \lt 0 \rightarrow(2,1) \text{ is a saddle point.}
					</me>
					<me>
						D(-2,1)=8-8 \cdot 1-4(-2)^2=-16 \lt 0 \rightarrow(-2,1) \text{ is a saddle point.}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Given the following information about two functions, <m>g(x)</m> and <m>f(x)</m>:
			</p>
			<p>
				Compute each of the following. Simplify your answers.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(3)=4</m> <m>f'(3)=-7</m> 
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>g(3)=2</m> <m>g'(3)=5</m> 
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(6)=8</m> <m>f'(6)=3</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>g(6)=9</m><m>g'(6)=10</m> 
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>h'(3)</m> if <m>h(x)=f(x)g(x)</m>. 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\amp h'(x)=f'(x) g(x)+f(x) g'(x) \\
						\amp h'(3)=(-7)(2)+(4)(5)=6
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>h'(6)</m> if <m>h(x)=\dfrac{3f(x)}{4}</m>. 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\amp h'(x)=\frac{3}{4} f'(x) \\
						\amp h'(6)=\frac{3}{4}(3)=\frac{9}{4}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>\displaystyle \lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{f(h+3)-f(3)}{h}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(h+3)-f(3)}{h}=f'(3)=-7
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The average value of <m>g'(x)</m> on <m>[3,6]</m>. 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\frac{1}{6-3} \int_3^6 g'(x) d x \amp =\left.\frac{1}{3} g(x)\right|_3 ^6 =\frac{1}{3}[g(6)-g(3)] \\
						\amp =\frac{1}{3}(9-2)=\frac{7}{3}
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The relative rate of change of <m>g(x)</m> at <m>x=3</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\frac{g'(3)}{g(3)}=\frac{5}{2}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
